<% content_for :title, "Big-O lab" %>

<header class="mb-3">
  <h1 class="mb-0">Big-O lab</h1>
  <p class="text-muted text-sm mb-0">
    Move the input size and watch the real operation counts. The time estimates
    assume roughly <%= number_to_human(@ops_per_second) %> simple operations per
    second — useful for intuition, not a benchmark.
  </p>
</header>

<section class="card bigo" data-controller="complexity"
         data-complexity-ops-per-second-value="<%= @ops_per_second %>">
  <div class="field">
    <label for="size-slider">
      Input size: <strong class="mono" data-complexity-target="size">1,000</strong> records
    </label>
    <input type="range" class="slider" id="size-slider" data-complexity-slider
           min="0" max="<%= @sizes.length - 1 %>" step="1" value="2"
           data-action="input->complexity#update"
           aria-describedby="size-help">
    <span class="field__hint" id="size-help">
      Steps through <%= @sizes.map { |s| number_with_delimiter(s) }.join(", ") %>.
    </span>
  </div>

  <div class="stack stack--tight mt-3">
    <% [["O(1)", "constant"], ["O(log n)", "log"], ["O(n)", "linear"],
        ["O(n log n)", "linearithmic"], ["O(n^2)", "quadratic"],
        ["O(2^n)", "exponential"]].each do |label, curve| %>
      <div class="bigo__row" data-complexity-target="row" data-curve="<%= curve %>">
        <span class="bigo__label"><%= label %></span>
        <div class="bigo__track">
          <div class="bigo__fill" data-fill style="width: 1%"></div>
        </div>
        <span class="bigo__value" data-value></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <div class="verdict verdict--correct mt-3" style="display: block;">
    <strong data-complexity-target="verdict"></strong>
  </div>

  <p class="text-xs text-faint mt-2 mb-0">
    Bars use a logarithmic scale — on a linear scale every curve except the
    worst would be invisible.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="card mt-3">
  <h2>Why this matters</h2>
  <div class="prose text-sm">
    <p>
      Big-O describes how work grows, not how fast something is now. An O(n)
      loop that makes a network call per element can be far slower than an
      O(n²) loop over integers in memory.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-0">
      Use it to answer one question: <strong>will this still work when the data
      is ten times bigger?</strong> Then measure.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
